course: dictionary
lesson: 7073
title: Patrol: going round to keep watch
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To patrol a place is to walk or drive around it again and again, checking that it is safe and that nothing is wrong. The clue words are the important ones: go around, check, safe. In the Rat's words, they are «watching over your house, patrolling your boundaries». Boundaries are the edges of a place, so patrolling them means going round the edges to keep watch.
explain: Patrol can also be a noun, a group of people who do the patrolling. Police, soldiers and Scouts can all form a patrol. In Scouts and Guides, a patrol is a small group of young people, so when you read the word, ask yourself whether it is a job (a verb) or a group of people (a noun).
explain: Look at the words around patrol to check your meaning. «At patrolled beaches, lifeguards put up red and yellow flags» tells you that the lifeguards watch the beach so that swimmers stay safe. Put your meaning into the sentence: lifeguards go around the beach to keep swimmers safe. It makes sense, so you are right.
fact: Patrol means to go around an area regularly to check that it is safe.
fact: Patrol can be a verb («lifeguards patrol the beach») or a noun («a patrol»). The verb changes: patrols, patrolled, patrolling.
fact: «On patrol» means busy patrolling, as a police officer or lifeguard might be.
fact: In the UK, school crossing patrols help children cross the road safely.
fact: Synonyms for patrol include guard and watch over, but patrol suggests going round an area, not staying in one spot.
exercise: «At patrolled beaches, lifeguards watch the swimmers.» What does patrolled mean here? | *Gone round regularly to check it is safe | Painted in red and yellow | Closed for the winter | Left empty and forgotten
exercise: Scouts split into small groups, and each group is called a patrol. What is a patrol in this sentence? | A long walk in the rain | A badge for swimming | A type of boat | *A small group
exercise: Find and copy the one word in this sentence that means go round regularly to check: «Some trained elephants help rangers patrol the forest.» | *patrol | trained | forest | elephants
test: The Rat says they are «patrolling your boundaries». What does patrolling mean? | Sleeping under a tree | Building a fence | Jumping over walls | *Going round the edges to keep watch
test: What does "on patrol" mean? | Lost and confused | On a holiday trip | *Busy patrolling | Asleep in bed
exam: Which sentence uses patrol as a noun? | Dogs patrol the yard every night. | The lifeguards patrol the beach. | They patrolled the park all morning. | *The Scout patrol set off at dawn to check the camp.
